  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nQuiet and low-impact weather will persist through late afternoon \nunder the influence of a mid-level ridge axis and lingering surface \nhigh pressure anchored over the eastern Great Lakes region. \nTemperatures remain just below typical late-August normals, \nwith afternoon highs peaking near 80F beneath scattered high \ncirrus.\n\nThe pattern transitions quickly tonight as low-level warm air \nadvection (WAA) and broad-scale isentropic ascent ramp up across\ncentral Illinois. An amplifying shortwave trough over the \nnorthern Plains will pivot eastward into the Upper Midwest \novernight, driving an increasing 30-35 kt 850 mb low-level jet \n(LLJ). Moisture transport focusing along the nose of this LLJ \nwill drive a corridor of theta-e advection and synoptic forcing \nacross the CWA. Recent convective- allowing models (CAMs) \ncontinue to trend more aggressive with nocturnal shower potential\novernight into early Wednesday morning. Forcing for ascent will\nbe further bolstered by a pre- frontal trough propagating into \nwestern Illinois after Midnight before reaching the I-57 \ncorridor toward daybreak. Expect scattered showers to focus \nprimarily north of the I-70 corridor through sunrise Wednesday.\nThe combination of expanding mid-level cloud cover, persistent \nWAA, and sporadic precipitation will limit nocturnal \nradiational cooling, holding overnight lows in the mid-to-upper \n60s.\n\nA more noticeable pattern shift arrives Wednesday afternoon as the \nshortwave trough and associated surface cold front track into the \nGreat Lakes. Moderate boundary layer destabilization is expected \nahead of the advancing front as a narrow plume of low-70s dewpoints \npools over the region, helping yield MLCAPE values around 2500 J/kg \nby mid-afternoon. Overlapping this buoyancy with ~30 knots of 0-6 km \nbulk shear could favor organized multi-cell and semi-discrete \nsupercellular structures along and ahead of the boundary. Deep- \nlayer shear vectors oriented largely parallel to the initiating \nfrontal zone alongside forecast mesoscale soundings featuring \nstraight or subtle veer-back-veer hodographs point to a large\nhail or damaging wind threat over an isolated tornado risk.\n\nCAM coverage signals remain somewhat mixed Wednesday afternoon. The \nprimary upper-level vort max will be displaced well to our north, \nleaving synoptic-scale forcing over central Illinois somewhat\npoor and potentially limiting total storm coverage.\n \nFurthermore, limited moisture return ahead of the front keeps \ntotal QPF low. With forecast PWATs around 1.5 inches and \nprogressive storm motion, the flash flooding risk from \nconvective rainfall is quite low.\n\nFollowing frontal passage Wednesday evening, cool and dry conditions \ntake hold for the end of the workweek into the weekend. An \nelongated Central U.S. upper ridge will build east, driving an \nexpansive surface high pressure system and a continental polar (cP) \nairmass across the Midwest and Great Lakes. Expect comfortable \nconditions with afternoon highs hovering right around 80F \nThursday and Friday. Lowering dewpoints into the 50s will \nsupport efficient radiational cooling overnight, dropping low \ntemperatures into the mid-to-upper 50s.\n\nBy early next week, medium-range global ensembles indicate a notable \nwarming trend taking shape. A strong upper-level trough making \nlandfall in the Pacific Northwest will amplify and nudge the central \nCONUS ridge axis into the Midwest. The combined presence of a \n588594 dam upper height dome and persistent low-level return \nflow on its western flank will drive highs back into the upper \n80s to low 90s. With dewpoints rebounding back into the upper \n60s to around 70F, maximum heat index values could approach \ntriple digits by Monday and Tuesday.",
